  "result": {
    "engine": "jupyter",
    "markdown": "---\ngd-head-tags:\n  - >-\n    `<script type=\"application/ld+json\">[{\"@context\": \"https://schema.org\",\n    \"@type\": \"SoftwareSourceCode\", \"name\": \"EdgeCase\", \"description\":\n    \"High-performance graph analysis library\", \"codeRepository\":\n    \"https://github.com/rich-iannone/edgecase\", \"license\":\n    \"https://opensource.org/licenses/MIT\", \"url\":\n    \"https://rich-iannone.github.io/edgecase\", \"documentation\":\n    \"https://rich-iannone.github.io/edgecase\", \"programmingLanguage\": {\"@type\":\n    \"ComputerLanguage\", \"name\": \"Python\"}}, {\"@context\": \"https://schema.org\",\n    \"@type\": \"WebPage\", \"name\": \"Graph.percolation_threshold_analysis -\n    EdgeCase\", \"isPartOf\": {\"@type\": \"WebSite\", \"name\": \"EdgeCase\"}, \"url\":\n    \"https://rich-iannone.github.io/edgecase/reference/Graph.percolation_threshold_analysis.html\"}]</script>`{=html}\nopen-graph:\n  title: >-\n    [Graph.percolation_threshold_analysis()]{.doc-object-name .doc-function\n    .doc-label .doc-label-method} | EdgeCase\n  description: Perform percolation threshold analysis.\ntwitter-card:\n  title: >-\n    [Graph.percolation_threshold_analysis()]{.doc-object-name .doc-function\n    .doc-label .doc-label-method} | EdgeCase\n  description: Perform percolation threshold analysis.\ntitle: >-\n  [Graph.percolation_threshold_analysis()]{.doc-object-name .doc-function\n  .doc-label .doc-label-method}\nbody-classes: doc-api-page doc-py-api-page\nshift-heading-level-by: 0\nbread-crumbs: false\ntoc-depth: 3\ndescription-meta: Perform percolation threshold analysis.\npage-navigation: false\nhtml-table-processing: none\n---\n\n::: {.doc-subject}\n\nPerform percolation threshold analysis.\n:::\n\n::: {.doc-usage-source}\nUsage\n\n[Source](https://github.com/rich-iannone/edgecase/blob/main/python/edgecase/graph.py#L59805-L59948){target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\"}\n:::\n\n::: {.doc-signature .doc-function}\n```pythonsignature\nGraph.percolation_threshold_analysis(\n    steps=20,\n    trials=10,\n    seed=None,\n)\n```\n:::\n\n::: {.doc-text}\n\n![Percolation threshold analysis](../assets/graphs/percolation-threshold-analysis.light.svg){.lightbox fig-alt=\"The two diamonds sharing hub D. The chart plots, for removal fractions q of 0, 0.25, 0.5, 0.75, and 1, the giant component's share of the surviving nodes, averaged over 10 random trials: 1.0, 0.82, 0.55, 0.75, 0. It dips at q = 0.5, where the graph tends to split, and is 0 once every node is gone.\" width=\"100%\"}\n\nSystematically removes nodes at evenly spaced fractions and measures the relative size of\nthe giant connected component (GCC) at each step. This helps identify the critical\npercolation threshold where the network fragments.\n\nFor each removal fraction `q = 0, 1/steps, 2/steps, ..., 1`, the method runs `trials=`\nindependent rounds of `node_percolation()`, measures each surviving graph with\n`largest_component_size()`, and divides by the number of surviving nodes. The average of\nthese ratios over the trials is reported for that `q`. A value of `1.0` means the\nsurvivors are still all in one piece; values near `0` mean they are scattered into many\nsmall fragments.\n\nUse it to:\n\n- estimate how much random node failure a network can absorb before it breaks apart\n- compare the robustness of different networks, or of one network before and after a\n  redesign\n- produce the data for a robustness curve (plot `q` against the returned GCC fraction)\n\nBecause the GCC is measured relative to the *surviving* nodes rather than the original\nnode count, the curve need not decrease monotonically: at high `q` only a handful of\nnodes remain, and even a small cluster can make up a large share of them. At `q=1.0`\nthere are no survivors and the value is `0.0`. Edge direction is ignored when finding\ncomponents (weak connectivity). The cost is `(steps + 1) * trials` percolation runs, each\nlinear in the size of the graph. The original graph is not modified.\n:::\n\n## Parameters {.doc-parameters}\n\n::: {.doc-definition-items}\n<code>[steps]{.doc-parameter-name}[:]{.doc-parameter-annotation-sep} [int]{.doc-parameter-annotation} [=]{.doc-parameter-default-sep .op} [[20]{.dv}]{.doc-parameter-default}</code>\n\n:   Number of intervals the range `[0, 1]` is divided into. Defaults to `20`, which tests\n    `q = 0.0, 0.05, ..., 1.0`. The result has `steps + 1` entries. Must be at least `1`;\n    `steps=0` raises a `ValueError`.\n\n<code>[trials]{.doc-parameter-name}[:]{.doc-parameter-annotation-sep} [int]{.doc-parameter-annotation} [=]{.doc-parameter-default-sep .op} [[10]{.dv}]{.doc-parameter-default}</code>\n\n:   Number of independent random removals performed at each `q`; the reported value is\n    their average. Defaults to `10`. More trials give a smoother, less noisy curve at\n    proportionally higher cost. With `trials=0` every value is `nan`.\n\n<code>[seed]{.doc-parameter-name}[:]{.doc-parameter-annotation-sep} [int | None]{.doc-parameter-annotation} [=]{.doc-parameter-default-sep .op} [None]{.doc-parameter-default}</code>\n\n:   Seed for the random number generator that drives all trials. The same graph,\n    `steps=`, `trials=`, and `seed=` always give the same curve. If `None` (default), a\n    fresh random seed is used and the results vary from call to call.\n:::\n\n## Returns {.doc-returns}\n\n::: {.doc-definition-items}\n<code> [list of tuple]{.doc-parameter-annotation}</code>\n\n:   List of `steps + 1` tuples `(q, relative_gcc_size)` where:\n\n    - `q` is the fraction of nodes removed\n    - `relative_gcc_size` is the average relative size of the giant connected component\n    (GCC size / surviving nodes)\n\n    For an empty graph, the result is `[(0.0, 0.0)]`.\n:::\n\n## Raises {.doc-raises}\n\n::: {.doc-definition-items}\n<code> [ValueError]{.doc-parameter-annotation}</code>\n\n:   If `steps=` is `0`.\n:::\n\n## Technical Notes {.doc-technical-notes}\n\nPercolation theory studies what fraction of nodes you can remove before a graph\nfragments; the *percolation threshold* $q_c$ is the fraction at which the giant\nconnected component vanishes. This routine samples the transition curve empirically\nby removing nodes at fractions $q = 0, \\tfrac{1}{s}, \\tfrac{2}{s}, \\dots, 1$ and\naveraging the giant-component fraction over `trials` random draws at each $q$.\nSharper transitions show up as steeper drops in the returned curve. For\nErdős-Rényi random graphs the critical value falls near\n$q_c \\approx 1 - 1/\\langle k \\rangle$ with $\\langle k \\rangle$ the average degree,\na benchmark useful for spot-checking whether an empirical network is more or less\nrobust than a random baseline.\n\n## See Also {.doc-see-also}\n\n[](`~node_percolation`) \n\n:   A single random removal of nodes.\n\n[](`~edge_percolation`) \n\n:   A single random removal of edges.\n\n[](`~largest_component_size`) \n\n:   The size of what holds together.\n\n## Examples {.doc-examples}\n\nA ring is about as fragile as a connected network can be: every removed node cuts the\ncycle, so at `q=0.2` the largest surviving piece already holds only about a third of the\nsurvivors:\n\n::: {#97373940 .cell execution_count=1}\n``` {.python .cell-code}\nimport edgecase as ec\n\nring = ec.Graph().add_cycle_graph(40)\n\nresults = ring.percolation_threshold_analysis(steps=5, trials=20, seed=1)\n[(q, round(gcc, 2)) for q, gcc in results]\n```\n\n::: {.cell-output .cell-output-display execution_count=1}\n```\n[(0.0, 1.0), (0.2, 0.34), (0.4, 0.27), (0.6, 0.23), (0.8, 0.26), (1.0, 0.0)]\n```\n:::\n:::\n\n\n### A club against a ring\n\nZachary's karate club has 34 members and 78 friendships. Compared with a ring of 34, it\nholds together far longer: with 30% of the members gone, the largest piece still holds\nover 90% of the survivors, against a third for the ring:\n\n::: {#e4a7f332 .cell execution_count=2}\n``` {.python .cell-code}\nimport polars as pl\n\nclub = ec.karate_club().percolation_threshold_analysis(\n    steps=10, trials=50, seed=1\n)\nring = ec.Graph().add_cycle_graph(34).percolation_threshold_analysis(\n    steps=10, trials=50, seed=1\n)\n\npl.DataFrame(\n    {\n        \"q\": [q for q, _ in club],\n        \"club\": [gcc for _, gcc in club],\n        \"ring\": [gcc for _, gcc in ring],\n    }\n).with_columns(pl.col(\"club\", \"ring\").round(2))\n```\n\n::: {.cell-output .cell-output-display execution_count=2}\n```{=html}\n<div><style>\n.dataframe > thead > tr,\n.dataframe > tbody > tr {\n  text-align: right;\n  white-space: pre-wrap;\n}\n</style>\n<small>shape: (11, 3)</small><table border=\"1\" class=\"dataframe\"><thead><tr><th>q</th><th>club</th><th>ring</th></tr><tr><td>f64</td><td>f64</td><td>f64</td></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>0.0</td><td>1.0</td><td>1.0</td></tr><tr><td>0.1</td><td>0.99</td><td>0.66</td></tr><tr><td>0.2</td><td>0.91</td><td>0.4</td></tr><tr><td>0.3</td><td>0.91</td><td>0.33</td></tr><tr><td>0.4</td><td>0.79</td><td>0.28</td></tr><tr><td>&hellip;</td><td>&hellip;</td><td>&hellip;</td></tr><tr><td>0.6</td><td>0.67</td><td>0.25</td></tr><tr><td>0.7</td><td>0.5</td><td>0.26</td></tr><tr><td>0.8</td><td>0.52</td><td>0.3</td></tr><tr><td>0.9</td><td>0.49</td><td>0.37</td></tr><tr><td>1.0</td><td>0.0</td><td>0.0</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n```\n:::\n:::\n\n\nEven with half the club gone, the largest piece holds about 80% of the 17 survivors, as\nin this draw, where it holds 13 and only four members are cut off:\n\n::: {#b1af1f66 .cell execution_count=3}\n``` {.python .cell-code}\n(\n    ec.karate_club()\n    .layout_kamada_kawai()\n    .node_percolation(q=0.5, seed=3)\n    .plot(layout=\"pos\", color_by=\"faction\", label_by=\"label\")\n)\n```\n\n::: {.cell-output .cell-output-display execution_count=3}\n```{=html}\n<div class=\"ec-theme\" style=\"display:contents\"><style class=\"ec-theme-vars\">:where(.ec-theme){--ect-3239c383:#fafafa;--ect-e5dfd507:#94a3b8;--ect-8a2de1d4:#374151;--ect-57425a7e:rgba(15,23,42,0.88);--ect-21ade003:#f1f5f9;--ect-61169ea5:#c2410c;--ect-f59ba8df:#a1a1aa}@media (prefers-color-scheme: dark){.ec-theme{--ect-3239c383:#0d1117;--ect-e5dfd507:#4b5563;--ect-8a2de1d4:#d1d5db;--ect-57425a7e:rgba(241,245,249,0.92);--ect-21ade003:#0f172a;--ect-61169ea5:#f97316;--ect-f59ba8df:#71717a}}:is(html.quarto-light, body.quarto-light, html[data-bs-theme=\"light\"], [data-jp-theme-light=\"true\"], body.vscode-light, body.vscode-high-contrast-light, html[theme=\"light\"]) .ec-theme{--ect-3239c383:#fafafa;--ect-e5dfd507:#94a3b8;--ect-8a2de1d4:#374151;--ect-57425a7e:rgba(15,23,42,0.88);--ect-21ade003:#f1f5f9;--ect-61169ea5:#c2410c;--ect-f59ba8df:#a1a1aa}:is(html.quarto-dark, body.quarto-dark, html[data-bs-theme=\"dark\"], [data-jp-theme-light=\"false\"], body.vscode-dark, body.vscode-high-contrast:not(.vscode-high-contrast-light), html[theme=\"dark\"]) .ec-theme{--ect-3239c383:#0d1117;--ect-e5dfd507:#4b5563;--ect-8a2de1d4:#d1d5db;--ect-57425a7e:rgba(241,245,249,0.92);--ect-21ade003:#0f172a;--ect-61169ea5:#f97316;--ect-f59ba8df:#71717a}</style><div class=\"ec-plot\" style=\"position:relative;width:fit-content;max-width:100%;margin:1em auto\">\n<style>.ec-plot > svg { display:block; max-width:100%; height:auto; }</style>\n<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" width=\"700\" height=\"500\" viewBox=\"39.9 14.0 689.2 492.3\" style=\"font-family: -apple-system, BlinkMacSystemFont, 'Segoe UI', Roboto, 'Helvetica Neue', Arial, sans-serif;\">\n<rect x=\"39.9\" y=\"14.0\" width=\"689.2\" height=\"492.3\" fill=\"var(--ect-3239c383)\"/>\n<line x1=\"480.1\" y1=\"66.9\" x2=\"431.2\" y2=\"90.0\" stroke=\"#4e79a7\" stroke-width=\"1.98\" stroke-opacity=\"0.4\" stroke-linecap=\"round\"/>\n<line x1=\"480.1\" y1=\"66.9\" x2=\"431.2\" y2=\"90.0\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"transparent\" stroke-width=\"8.0\" pointer-events=\"stroke\" data-edge=\"17-7-0\" data-attrs='{\"id\": 41, \"edge\": \"6 \\u2014 16\"}'/>\n<line x1=\"276.2\" y1=\"254.7\" x2=\"244.5\" y2=\"255.7\" stroke=\"#f28e2c\" stroke-width=\"